# Runs inside pacman (PKGBUILD's `install=`). It prints what changed and how to
# switch the login chain; it enables nothing and starts nothing.
#
# A package that silently took display-manager.service from an existing desktop
# would be a surprise, and the boot chain is the administrator's decision. The
# same reason packaging/install.sh does not enable the chain without `--enable`.
#
# Written in English because pacman prints these lines to a terminal that may not
# be UTF-8 (e.g. during `pacstrap`).

post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'

Inox is installed but not enabled. The binaries are in /usr/bin, the three PAM
stacks are in /etc/pam.d, the units are in /usr/lib/systemd/system, and the
`greeter` system account is created by pacman's systemd-sysusers hook (check it
with `id greeter`).

Nothing starts a login screen yet.

  Try the login chain on a spare VT (does not touch the boot chain):
      sudo systemctl start inox-greeter@4.service

  Make Inox the boot-time login screen (disable any other display manager first):
      sudo systemctl disable --now sddm.service   # or gdm.service, greetd.service, ...
      sudo ln -sf /usr/lib/systemd/system/inox-greeter@1.service \
          /etc/systemd/system/display-manager.service
      sudo reboot

  Undo the boot-chain switch:
      sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/display-manager.service
      sudo systemctl enable <your previous display manager>

  Read the logs:
      journalctl -u inox-auth -u inox-greeter@1

Before switching the screen, read the deployment notes:
    https://github.com/Inox-desktop/inox/blob/main/docs/book/src/usage/login.md

This package is a build recipe, not a supported distribution channel: the login
chain has no real-hardware acceptance result, and AUR builds are not CI-produced
binaries (docs/book/src/development/release.md).
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}
